Port HEVC: PR #44's h265.c on this base, plus the four sites it needs

HEVC now decodes bit-exact on the VE through stock ffmpeg. H.264 is

HEVC now decodes bit-exact on the VE through stock ffmpeg -- all three
vectors, WPP and non-WPP -- and H.264 is unregressed at 5/5.

    h01-640x480-main    PASS (va) bit-exact, ve+25
    h02-1280x720-main   PASS (va) bit-exact, ve+25
    h03-640x480-nowpp   PASS (va) bit-exact, ve+25

src/h265.c is taken verbatim from bootlin PR #44 (head c488d8df, "Kernel
6.18" by Cn8001), which ports HEVC to the stabilized V4L2 stateless uAPI --
V4L2_CID_STATELESS_HEVC_* names, flags-bitfield SPS/PPS/slice_params, a new
h265_fill_decode_params(), and the reference-picture-set restructure that
maps VA-API's ReferenceFrames[] into poc_st_curr_before/after[] and
poc_lt_curr[]. That last part carried the real design risk and is correct.

PR #44 cannot decode anything on cedrus by itself -- H.264 or HEVC --
because it is based on master and lacks this branch's format-ordering fix.
Both codecs fail identically with the CAPTURE format clamped to 16x16 and
then EBUSY on the OUTPUT format, which is the V4L2 stateless spec's
mandated order (dev-decoder.rst, "Initialization") enforced deliberately by
cedrus_s_fmt_vid_out(). This branch already sets the coded format first, in
RequestCreateSurfaces2.

THE THREE BUGS FIXED HERE, each found by measurement rather than reading:

1. The Annex-B start code was prepended based on UNINITIALISED MEMORY.
   context->h264_start_code is never assigned anywhere: h264_get_controls(),
   which would set it, is defined and never called. H.264 had been decoding
   bit-exact purely because the heap happened to hold zero there. cedrus
   accepts only START_CODE_NONE, for BOTH codecs -- .max and .def are NONE
   for V4L2_CID_STATELESS_H264_START_CODE and the HEVC one alike -- so the
   flag is now set explicitly to false. This is what fixed the non-WPP
   stall, and it also makes H.264's previously-lucky behaviour deliberate.

2. nal_unit_type was read from that start code, yielding 0 (TRAIL_N, a
   NON-reference picture) instead of the real type. cedrus passes it
   straight to the hardware in VE_DEC_H265_DEC_NAL_HDR_NAL_UNIT_TYPE().
   Fixed by skipping a start code before reading the NAL header, which is
   still correct now that we no longer add one -- a caller that does will
   not be misparsed.

3. The coded pixelformat was hardcoded to V4L2_PIX_FMT_H264_SLICE in
   surface.c, so an HEVC stream set S264 on the OUTPUT queue, cedrus
   selected its H.264 codec ops, and the engine ran producing an all-zero
   frame. It now comes from driver_data->coded_pixelformat, recorded at
   RequestCreateConfig because the format must be set before any buffer
   exists -- earlier than the context that knows the profile.

Plus the sites our H.264-only patch had stripped HEVC from: meson.build
(h265.c was never compiled), three in config.c (CreateConfig refused the
profile, QueryConfigProfiles never advertised it, and
QueryConfigEntrypoints returned zero entrypoints, which is why vainfo kept
omitting HEVC after the first two were fixed), and four in picture.c.

KNOWN LIMITATIONS, stated rather than hidden:

  * HEVC scaling lists are not passed. PR #44 dropped the iqmatrix handling
    and never sets V4L2_CID_STATELESS_HEVC_SCALING_MATRIX. Our vectors have
    scaling_list_enabled = 0 and cedrus gates the write on that SPS flag, so
    the gate cannot see this. A stream that uses scaling lists will decode
    wrong.
  * Entry point offsets are not passed either. ffmpeg reports
    num_entry_point_offsets = 0 for WPP streams, so VA-API does not convey
    them at all; h01/h02 decode bit-exact regardless, because entry points
    let a decoder parallelise WPP substreams rather than being needed for
    correctness.
  * Two PPS flag bits are wrong -- DEBLOCKING_FILTER_CONTROL_PRESENT is set
    when the stream says 0 (#44 derives it instead of reading it, and
    VA-API does not expose the field), and UNIFORM_SPACING is never set.
    Neither is referenced anywhere in cedrus, so both are inert here and
    left alone rather than guessed at.
  * h264_start_code being hardcoded false is right for cedrus and wrong in
    general. Querying the per-codec START_CODE control is the correct form.

MPEG-2 stays unadvertised: RequestCreateConfig still refuses it, and
advertising what vaCreateConfig rejects is worse than advertising nothing.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
